A||N||5||DaveL||Throttled Valve Cable||||5||
Z||000000||DaveL||12-15-2000||03:59 PM||||Ok, I got a new cable for my 87 SE 2.5 DIS with a/t, and I found the infamous throttle valve cable button. When I push the button in, I can pull the half of the cable farthest from the tranny out about 1/4 to 1/2 inch. So what exactly gets adjusted during this process? When you turn the pulley by the TBI to W.O.T., it turns a lot more than 1/2".||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||12-15-2000||06:23 PM||||As you've seen the cable only pulls out a little ways. that's all you need. <P>When I adjust one of these I pull it out to expose about an 1/8-3/16 of the toothed sleeve. You'll see.... that's all you need if the cable is installed and working right. Most of the ones I've seen end up with top of the toothed sleeve close to even with the adjuster shell. A few teeth in or out. (By top I mean the top of the toothed fat part. Not any of the thiner area above that.)<P>The way it works is when you turn the pully, and thus pull out the cable, it only pulls out so far. When it hits the limit of travel it pulls the toothed sleeve into the adjuster.<P>It takes considerable force to do this. DO NOT press the gas pedal to make the adjustment. You can break the accel cable or bend the pedal hardware.<P>Usually to get to WOT you need to hand adjust the sleeve into the adjuster a few more teeth by hand. It won't quite go there by turning the wheel. At least I don't have that much grip to do it..... Usually an aditional 1-2/32 adjustment by hand, sleeve into the adjuster, is enough.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 12-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||DaveL||12-16-2000||11:10 AM||||Would it hurt to have some one put some pressure on the gas pedal while turning the pulley out to W.O.T.? I understand you can't exert enough pressure on the pedal to make the adjustment without risking damage to the throttle cable and/or assembly, but I can't help but wonder.||||reg||5
Z||000003||theogre||12-16-2000||12:19 PM||||I wouldn't do it. it's not worth the chance of wrecking it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||afRaceR||12-16-2000||03:14 PM||||The downside to this is that the pully on the cable may turn farther than the pedal would actually travel which would throw the transmission out of wack. You only want to pull it out as far as the pedal would pull it plus one or two clicks if needed.<P>I used to work in a transmission shop and this is the way we do it and have never had a problem. Unless the Fiero has an extremely weak cable or the pedal is weak, I can't see how there would be a problem.<P>If you feel like this would be a problem when you are adjusting, adjust it at the pulley a little at a time until the pedal won't put anymore pressure on the cable.<P>This cable needs to be set pretty exactly to make your transmission shift right, get it wrong and you'll be rebuilding it before you need to. Adjusting it by the pulley is not 100% accurate.||||reg||1
Z||000005||DaveL||12-18-2000||07:50 AM||||Finally, problem completely solved (knock on wood). A/T shifting perfectly, all the time, for two days now. I bought a new cable from the Fiero Store, and after seeing how the adjuster works, I readjusted the old one, and voila! it works! They sent me a GM replacement, which is good. Cheaper than the local dealer too. Now, I don't relly need it. Guess I'll save it just in case the old one ever does break. Anyone want to buy one?? {Throttle valve cable for 87 SE 2.5 a/t)<BR>I just can't believe I drove the thing around for 2 years with occasional delayed shifts! Damn, I bet I brought an eventual rebuild a few thousand miles sooner! Such a simple thing. Also, I did end up having my son put moderate pressure on the pedal while I turned the wheel, until he yelled the pedal was on the floor. Worked like a charm, he said there wasn't really a lot of resistance. I would bet adjusting it the other way and being sure is a lot easier than replacing the entire throttle cable. Some older cables would probably break.||||reg||10
